# Test: IS-IS redistribution config surface (spec-isis-11 redistribution wiring).
#
# VALIDATES: spec-isis-11-redistribution -- IS-IS meshes with BGP (and connected /
#            static) through Ze's protocol-agnostic redistribution framework in
#            BOTH directions:
#            - `redistribute { destination bgp { import isis } }` is accepted: the
#              SINGLE source name `isis` is registered with the source registry
#              (registerISIS -> RegisterISISSources, at init) so the
#              `redistribute-source` validator/completion accept it (AC-1, AC-2),
#              and validation works regardless of registration order (AC-9, the
#              source is registered before `ze config validate` runs the validator).
#            - `redistribute { destination isis { import connected/static/bgp } }`
#              is accepted: `destination isis` is a runtime-validated free-form list
#              key (no YANG schema change, A-1) and the IS-IS consumer (`isis`) is
#              the destination (AC-3/AC-4/AC-5 config surface).
#            - `redistribute { destination isis { import isis } }` validates at the
#              schema level (isis is a registered source) and is a no-op at runtime:
#              the generic loop-prevention evaluator rejects origin == importing
#              protocol because the single source name `isis` == the consumer name
#              `isis` (AC-10).
# PREVENTS:  a regression where the `isis` source name is NOT registered (so
#            `import isis` is rejected by the redistribute-source validator) or
#            `destination isis` is not accepted as a runtime list key.
#
# The LIVE route flow -- an IS-IS SPF route appearing in the BGP RIB (AC-1), and a
# connected/static/BGP prefix appearing as a TLV 135 entry in an IS-IS LSP and in a
# peer's RIB (AC-3/AC-4/AC-5/AC-6) -- needs raw L2 (AF_PACKET) on a Linux veth and
# is covered by:
#   - the unit tests in internal/plugins/isis/redistribute (producer emit ->
#     redistevents batch -> orchestrator dispatch shape; consumer InjectRoute ->
#     TLV 135 PrefixInfo; WithdrawRoute removal; up/down bit; self-import rejection;
#     metric boundary), and
#   - the QEMU / FRR isisd interop scenario `isis-redist-frr` (isis-13), which
#     proves FRR installs the redistributed reachability.
# This functional test exercises the CONFIG WIRING only: config file -> YANG schema
# -> redistribute source/destination validation.

# --- Both directions: import isis into BGP, and connected/static/bgp into isis. ---
stdin=isis-redist-both:terminator=EOF_BOTH
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
				remote 65533
			}
		}
	}
}

isis {
	net 49.0001.0000.0000.0001.00
	level l1-l2
	interfaces {
		interface eth0 {
			metric 10
			level l1-l2
		}
		interface eth1 {
			passive true
		}
	}
}

connected {
}

redistribute {
	destination bgp {
		import isis
	}
	destination isis {
		import connected
		import static
		import bgp
	}
}
EOF_BOTH

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze config validate -:stdin=isis-redist-both:exit=0
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=configuration valid

# --- Self-import: destination isis { import isis } validates (schema) and is a ----
# --- runtime no-op via loop prevention (single source name == consumer name). -----
stdin=isis-redist-self:terminator=EOF_SELF
isis {
	net 49.0001.0000.0000.0001.00
	level l1-l2
}

redistribute {
	destination isis {
		import isis
	}
}
EOF_SELF

cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze config validate -:stdin=isis-redist-self:exit=0
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=configuration valid
